<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Florestan I, Prince of Monaco</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1785">1785</a>–<a href="/years/1856">1856</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Florestan I was Prince of Monaco from 1841 to 1856, a younger son of Honoré IV who had earlier worked as an actor in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>. His government, largely run by his wife Caroline Gibert, imposed monopolies and taxes that provoked the revolt of 1848, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/menton">Menton</a> and Roquebrune broke away under the protection of the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardinia">Sardinia</a>. Monaco never regained them.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/florestan-i-prince-of-monaco.jpg" alt="Florestan I, Prince of Monaco" width="320" height="393" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:FlorestanI-1-.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
